Cisco published CVE-2026-20245 on 2026-06-04, a command injection in the CLI of Catalyst SD-WAN Controller, Manager and Validator that lets an authenticated, local attacker with netadmin rights run commands as root (CVSS 7.8); the netadmin rights can come from valid credentials or from exploiting CVE-2026-20182 or CVE-2026-20127, Cisco observed limited cases of configuration being pushed to edge devices, and there is no workaround ([Cisco, 2026-06-04](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-privesc-4uxFrdzx)). The first fixed releases are 20.9.9.2, 20.12.7.2, 20.15.4.5, 20.15.5.3, 20.18.3.1 and 26.1.1.2 ([Cisco, 2026-06-04](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-privesc-4uxFrdzx)). CISA added it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-06-09 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-06-09](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)). Mandiant found it exploited as a zero-day in April 2026 at a service provider: the attacker, already connected to the SD-WAN Manager as `vmanage-admin` through rogue peering and SSH sessions, uploaded a crafted tenant list through the CLI to gain root, created a root-privileged account named `troot`, reached it from the admin account with `su`, and later deleted the files it created and restored the configuration it had changed ([Mandiant, 2026-06-24](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/zero-day-exploitation-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-manager)). Mandiant says the earlier connections possibly came from exploiting CVE-2026-20127 or CVE-2026-20182 and names no actor ([Mandiant, 2026-06-24](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/zero-day-exploitation-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-manager)).

**Exposure:** Catalyst SD-WAN Controller, Manager and Validator below the fixed release of their train, which matters most where netadmin access is reachable by an attacker, including through the unauthenticated CVE-2026-20182 ([Cisco, 2026-06-04](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-privesc-4uxFrdzx)).

**Detection:** new UID-0 accounts in the SD-WAN Manager's local account files, `su` from the admin account to an unknown account, and CLI tenant-upload commands nobody ran ([Mandiant, 2026-06-24](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/zero-day-exploitation-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-manager)).

**Defender takeaway:** the attacker cleaned up after itself, so on a Manager that ran an affected release, compare local accounts and configuration with a known-good baseline rather than relying on finding the uploaded files.
